#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Remeasure a headline row of every package against its Python twin (the
viewer's against the upstream Node backend), and fail when Haskell does not
win most of the interleaved repetitions.

    scripts/bench-gate                      # every entry below
    scripts/bench-gate --shard 2/4          # every fourth entry, from the second (CI's matrix;
                                            # the viewer's entry runs in CI's viewer job instead)
    scripts/bench-gate PKG [PKG ...]        # only these packages' entries
    scripts/bench-gate --list               # the entries and their rows
    options: --reps N (default 5), --no-build, --json FILE

CI runs it only on a dispatch with ``bench`` (the ``bench-gate`` job, and
the ``viewer`` job for petrus-viewer-server): the figures need minutes of
machine time and a quiet runner. The committed results files (``packages/*/bench/*.md``) are
measured by hand, on a quiet machine, with more repetitions; this gate is the
check that their headline rows still hold for the code at HEAD.

**What it measures.** ENTRIES names, for every package with a Python twin,
one or more rows, chosen where the margin is thinnest (the rows a regression
would push below 1.0× first) and, for a package without a thin row, its
headline row. A *pair* entry runs the package's benchmark executable
(selected to its rows with tasty's ``-p``; tasty-bench mean in wall-clock
time, ``--stdev 5``, ``+RTS -M2G``) and the Python twin's command (the one
in the package's rerun block, restricted to the rows when the twin can be),
back to back, REPS times, alternating which side goes first. Each
repetition gives a ratio, Python over Haskell, from the two figures measured
next to each other; the row's ratio is the median of those. A *table* entry
runs a harness that interleaves both sides itself (21 runs or 5 rounds) and
prints a Markdown table; the row's ratio and its "k of n" count of the runs
or rounds Haskell won are read from the row's cells.

**When it fails.** A row passes when Haskell won more than half of its
repetitions (runs, rounds): "won k of n" with 2k > n, in one measurement,
with no second attempt. Before, the median of the repetitions decided, and a
row below 1.0 was measured again; a row whose two sides do the same server or
kernel work came out on either side of 1.0 from one run to the next, and the
rerun only gave the noise a second draw. Counting wins asks what the gate is
for (is Haskell faster, run for run, on the same machine at the same moment),
and a load spike costs one repetition, not the verdict. The gate also fails
when a row could not be measured or read. Every row's figures are printed as
a Markdown table, which also goes to ``$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`` in CI.

**Reading the figures.** Haskell figures are tasty-bench means (or the
``twin NAME NANOSECONDS`` lines of rows timed with the twin's protocol, or a
custom harness's own ``NAME VALUE UNIT`` lines); Python figures are what the
twin prints, read as scripts/bench-compare reads them (its ``figures``). Each
entry names both sides' rows explicitly.

petrus-history-store's ``jsonl.append-one-batch`` is the row the final
audit's FA-V-1 found 1.7× slower (its encoder held every record's
``Builder``); its Haskell-only ``diagnostic.encode-payload`` row, the
encoder alone, has no twin to compare with.

Left out on purpose, with the package's next-thinnest row gated instead:
rows whose time is the same kernel or server work on both sides, which the
runner measured on either side of 1.0x from run to run (single-repetition
noise, not the port). They are petrus-sqlite's
``tx.immediate-insert-commit`` and petrus-posix's
``atomic.write-1KiB-fsync`` (one ``fsync`` per call on both sides: strace
counts the same syncs, and the twin's SQLite syncs with ``fsync`` too;
0.89-1.08x and 0.96-1.12x over five runs), and petrus-dst's
``joined.one isolated database`` (``CREATE DATABASE``, the schemas and
``DROP DATABASE``, all server time; 0.97-1.02x). petrus-execution's
``archive/one-large/extract`` is out too: on the runner both sides spend
nearly all of it in ``write(2)`` (a bare 256 MiB write loop takes 58-60 ms
there, the extractions 57-59 ms), and Haskell came out 0.88-0.98x in five
runs (1.14x on the Apple M4), with 64 KiB or 128 KiB writes alike;
``archive/medium/extract`` is gated in its place. Their local figures are in
the packages' results files.

petrus-viewer-server's twin is the upstream Node backend: ``bench/run.mjs``
runs both (and needs ``pnpm install`` and ``pnpm build`` in
upstream/petrus-viewer), so CI runs that entry in its ``viewer`` job, which
has them, and ``--shard`` leaves it out. Its rows are read from run.mjs's
per-round table (Haskell's best target over Node's best, round by round).

Left out: the second harness of packages that have two
(petrus-agenticus-core's custody mode, petrus-engine-file's process rows,
the load modes of petrus-observation-http and petrus-simulation), whose other
rows are gated. Entries whose benchmarks read ``PETRUS_TEST_POSTGRES`` run
under scripts/with-postgres when it is unset.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import csv
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import statistics
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
TMP = ROOT / "bench-results" / "tmp" / "gate"
sys.dont_write_bytecode = True


def petrus(package: str, script: str = "python_bench.py", arguments: str = "") -> str:
    return (f'(cd upstream/petrus && "$(../../scripts/upstream-python)" -B ../../packages/{package}/bench/{script}'
            + (f" {arguments}" if arguments else "") + ")")


def hamsterdan(package: str, script: str = "bench.py", arguments: str = "") -> str:
    return (f'"$(scripts/upstream-python hamsterdan)" -I -B packages/{package}/bench/{script}'
            + (f" {arguments}" if arguments else ""))


def engine(package: str, script: str = "python_bench.py") -> str:
    return f'"$(scripts/upstream-python petrus-engine)" -B packages/{package}/bench/{script}'


def pair(package: str, rows: List[Tuple[str, str]], python: str, only: Optional[str] = None,
         haskell: Optional[List[str]] = None, tmpdir: bool = False) -> dict:
    """ROWS: (Haskell name, Python name). Haskell names are tasty paths
    ("All.group.row") or the names a custom harness prints. ONLY sets
    PETRUS_BENCH_ONLY for twins timed by scripts/bench_timing.py."""
    return {"kind": "pair", "package": package, "rows": rows, "python": python, "only": only,
            "haskell": haskell or [], "tmpdir": tmpdir}


def table(package: str, rows: List[str], command: str, sharded: bool = True) -> dict:
    """ROWS: regular expressions, each matching one line of the table COMMAND
    prints, with a ratio cell and a "k of n" cell (the runs Haskell won).
    SHARDED False: --shard leaves the entry out (CI runs it elsewhere)."""
    return {"kind": "table", "package": package, "rows": rows, "command": command, "sharded": sharded}


